# upload.sh is stored with LF, but this repo is worked on with core.autocrlf
# true, so a fresh clone on Windows would check it out with CRLF and bash would
# fail on the carriage returns -- halfway through a release, in the confusing way
# that a mis-resolved log name used to. Shell scripts keep LF everywhere.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Logs are release artifacts: upload.sh copies the working copy to S3, not the
# committed blob, so the published bytes are whatever is on disk. This line only
# settles what git stores. What a log is written with is settled by
# dev/verify/run-step.sh, which strips the CR that the Windows CRT adds on the
# way into its pipe; the two together are what make one run publish one set of
# bytes. Keeping only this line meant the bytes depended on whether anyone had
# happened to check the log out again since it was written.
*.log text eol=lf
